1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:02,446
Nobody had touched that ad in days.

2
00:00:02,446 --> 00:00:07,238
Go back to Section zero-point-three for a moment: the whole course opened on one

3
00:00:07,238 --> 00:00:11,850
claim, nobody can reliably pick a winning ad by looking at it, including you.

4
00:00:11,850 --> 00:00:13,168
Testing finds winners.

5
00:00:13,168 --> 00:00:14,006
Taste doesn't.

6
00:00:14,006 --> 00:00:18,618
As of this recording, Meta and TikTok have built that exact belief into their

7
00:00:18,618 --> 00:00:19,636
delivery systems.

8
00:00:19,636 --> 00:00:24,368
Meta's Advantage+ suite and TikTok's Smart+ don't just run the test you set up.

9
00:00:24,368 --> 00:00:29,040
They generate their own variations of your creative, headlines swapped, images

10
00:00:29,040 --> 00:00:33,891
cropped differently, text repositioned, and quietly test those against each other

11
00:00:33,891 --> 00:00:36,467
too, often without a single click from you.

12
00:00:36,467 --> 00:00:37,784
That's not a footnote.

13
00:00:37,784 --> 00:00:40,899
It changes what "your ad" even means once it's live.

14
00:00:40,899 --> 00:00:42,516
Sit with that for a second.

15
00:00:42,516 --> 00:00:47,128
Why would you spend five sections building your own testing discipline if the

16
00:00:47,128 --> 00:00:50,602
platform's already doing something similar underneath you?

17
00:00:50,602 --> 00:00:52,698
Because "similar" isn't "the same".

18
00:00:52,698 --> 00:00:56,232
The platform's automation optimises the pieces you hand it.

19
00:00:56,232 --> 00:01:01,263
It has no idea which pieces are worth handing it in the first place, no view of your

20
00:01:01,263 --> 00:01:06,234
claims boundary, and no concept of which angle actually deserves to be in the batch

21
00:01:06,234 --> 00:01:06,654
at all.

22
00:01:06,654 --> 00:01:09,828
It's very good at finding a winner among your inputs.

23
00:01:09,828 --> 00:01:13,362
It has no opinion about whether those inputs were any good.

24
00:01:13,912 --> 00:01:17,759
Notice the shared move underneath two differently branded systems.

25
00:01:17,759 --> 00:01:22,539
Both platforms take the raw materials you hand them, not just one finished ad, and

26
00:01:22,539 --> 00:01:27,086
recombine them on your behalf, then let performance decide which recombination

27
00:01:27,086 --> 00:01:27,611
survives.

28
00:01:27,611 --> 00:01:32,508
That's the same discipline Section 3.6's workshop taught you to do by hand: generate

29
00:01:32,508 --> 00:01:36,821
a real batch, not one hero asset, and let a test sort winners from losers.

30
00:01:36,821 --> 00:01:41,485
The platforms have simply moved a version of that step inside their own delivery

31
00:01:41,485 --> 00:01:46,265
systems, running underneath the testing architecture you build yourself in Section

32
00:01:46,265 --> 00:01:46,614
seven.

33
00:01:46,614 --> 00:01:48,771
Here's what actually changes for you.

34
00:01:48,771 --> 00:01:52,211
Your job used to stop at "which of my twenty variants won".

35
00:01:52,211 --> 00:01:57,107
Now it includes a second layer: which sub-variation the platform generated from your

36
00:01:57,107 --> 00:02:01,129
winner is actually running, and whether it still says what you meant.

37
00:02:01,129 --> 00:02:05,968
Winning at the level you control doesn't guarantee the exact thing a viewer sees is

38
00:02:05,968 --> 00:02:07,775
still the ad you signed off on.

39
00:02:07,775 --> 00:02:12,497
That doesn't shrink your job, it adds a layer underneath the one you already had.

40
00:02:12,497 --> 00:02:15,470
You still decide which angles are worth generating.

41
00:02:15,470 --> 00:02:19,900
The platform just took over one narrower decision, which wording combination

42
00:02:19,900 --> 00:02:20,716
performs best.

43
00:02:21,266 --> 00:02:25,141
Worth checking on your own account before you assume anything.

44
00:02:25,141 --> 00:02:30,016
Individual enhancements toggle separately, ad by ad, not as one all-or-nothing

45
00:02:30,016 --> 00:02:30,454
switch.

46
00:02:30,454 --> 00:02:31,704
That control exists.

47
00:02:31,704 --> 00:02:34,391
Most people never open the panel to use it.

48
00:02:34,391 --> 00:02:39,579
The line that matters most sits at the bottom of this table, and it doesn't move no

49
00:02:39,579 --> 00:02:41,767
matter how automated the rest gets.

50
00:02:41,767 --> 00:02:46,579
Nothing in Meta's or TikTok's creative automation checks a generated headline

51
00:02:46,579 --> 00:02:48,204
against a claims boundary.

52
00:02:48,204 --> 00:02:53,267
If Advantage+ combines your text in a way that drifts past what Section 6.2 would

53
00:02:53,267 --> 00:02:58,392
sign off on, that drift ships exactly as fast as everything else it's testing, and

54
00:02:58,392 --> 00:03:00,955
nobody catches it unless you're checking.

55
00:03:00,955 --> 00:03:04,267
What would that drift actually look like in practice?

56
00:03:04,267 --> 00:03:09,393
Picture Advantage+ pulling one phrase from your primary text and pairing it with a

57
00:03:09,393 --> 00:03:14,580
headline written for a different angle entirely, a combination that, read together,

58
00:03:14,580 --> 00:03:18,268
states something stronger than either piece did on its own.

59
00:03:18,268 --> 00:03:20,893
Neither half is new copy you didn't write.

60
00:03:20,893 --> 00:03:26,081
The combination is, and combinations are exactly what a claims review has never had

61
00:03:26,081 --> 00:03:29,456
to check before this generation of automation existed.

62
00:03:29,456 --> 00:03:32,643
Worth flagging precisely because it's easy to miss.

63
00:03:32,643 --> 00:03:37,644
A human copywriter combining two lines would notice the overreach instinctively.

64
00:03:37,644 --> 00:03:42,519
An optimisation system scoring purely on click-through has no reason to notice

65
00:03:42,519 --> 00:03:43,081
anything.

66
00:03:43,081 --> 00:03:44,581
It isn't being careless.

67
00:03:44,581 --> 00:03:46,581
It was never asked the question.

68
00:03:47,131 --> 00:03:52,097
One honest limit before this goes any further, and it's the same one that's run

69
00:03:52,097 --> 00:03:53,668
under this entire course.

70
00:03:53,668 --> 00:03:58,885
A generation tool, whether it's the one making your statics or the one now remixing

71
00:03:58,885 --> 00:04:03,473
your headlines inside an ad account, optimises for the metric it's given.

72
00:04:03,473 --> 00:04:06,239
Performance, in this case, not truthfulness.

73
00:04:06,239 --> 00:04:11,267
It has no model of Section two's claims boundary and no reason to check for one.

74
00:04:11,267 --> 00:04:16,295
That checking is still entirely a human job, yours, on a cadence, not a one-time

75
00:04:16,295 --> 00:04:18,558
setup step you configure and forget.

76
00:04:18,558 --> 00:04:23,712
Practically, that means pulling the actual delivered creative report periodically,

77
00:04:23,712 --> 00:04:28,678
not just your uploaded originals, and reading it the way you'd read a generated

78
00:04:28,678 --> 00:04:33,140
static in Section 3.3: does this still say what Dana would sign off on?

79
00:04:33,140 --> 00:04:37,980
Most weeks, the answer will be yes, because most recombinations are harmless.

80
00:04:37,980 --> 00:04:40,431
The check exists for the week it isn't.

81
00:04:40,981 --> 00:04:43,739
This section closes next lecture with a recap.

82
00:04:43,739 --> 00:04:48,475
But the honest note to carry forward is this one: the machine you've spent five

83
00:04:48,475 --> 00:04:52,851
sections learning to build by hand is now partly built into the platforms

84
00:04:52,851 --> 00:04:53,510
themselves.

85
00:04:53,510 --> 00:04:56,028
That doesn't make your judgement optional.

86
00:04:56,028 --> 00:05:00,464
It makes checking what the automation actually did the newest item on your

87
00:05:00,464 --> 00:05:01,423
pre-flight list.

88
00:05:01,423 --> 00:05:06,279
How would you know if this were happening right now, on your own account, without

89
00:05:06,279 --> 00:05:08,677
you noticing, the way Sam almost didn't?

90
00:05:08,677 --> 00:05:10,235
Open Ads Manager and look.

91
00:05:10,235 --> 00:05:14,911
If you've never checked which creative enhancements are switched on for a live

92
00:05:14,911 --> 00:05:19,887
campaign, that's the actual homework this lecture is leaving you with, ahead of the

93
00:05:19,887 --> 00:05:22,405
B05 checklist Section six builds properly.
